<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaidu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1235–<a href="/years/1301">1301</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaidu was a Mongol prince, a grandson of Ögedei, who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from 1272 to 1301. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>, he waged long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>. His opposition helped divide the Mongol world into competing khanates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaidu.jpg" alt="Kaidu" width="192" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maître de la Mazarine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qaidu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kaidu was the posthumous son of the Mongol prince Kashin, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a>, and so belonged to the House of Ögedei. Ögedei died in 1241 and was followed by his widow Töregene as regent, then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan">Güyük Khan</a>. In 1251 the throne passed to a Toluid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a>, and Kaidu, then about 16, was one of the few Ögedeid princes the new regime favoured. Möngke invested him with Qayaliq, southeast of Lake Balkhash, in 1252.</p><p>Despite this favour, Kaidu arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s envoy, the judge Shi Tianlin, in 1256 and held him for twenty years. In the civil war of 1260 to 1264 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> he is said to have supported Ariq Böke, though this is not certain. Excluded from Kublai's partition of the Mongol territories, he entered a long conflict with Kublai and Kublai's Ilkhanid allies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>'s defeat in 1264, Kublai summoned Kaidu to court, but Kaidu stayed away. In 1268 Kublai incited Baraq, khan of the Chagatai, to attack him. Baraq won at first, but Kaidu, helped by Möngke-Temür of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, defeated him on the Jaxartes. Peace was made at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>, with two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> going to Baraq. Kaidu then encouraged Baraq to invade Ilkhanid Persia, where he was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> on 22 July 1270 and died in August 1271. The Chagatayid princes submitted to Kaidu.</p><p>In 1282 Kaidu installed Baraq's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> as khan of the Chagatai Ulus, and the two cooperated for two decades. A princes' revolt against Kublai in 1276 freed Kaidu to deal with opposition in the west. With Duwa he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beshbaliq">Beshbaliq</a> in 1286, forcing Kublai's troops out of Uighuria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and in 1289 he briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> before retreating before Kublai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kublai's commanders Bayan and Tuq Tuqa then won reverses against Kaidu. After Kublai's death in 1294, his successor Temür Öljeitü Khan turned more forces against him. In 1300 Temür's nephew Qayishan defeated Kaidu, and on 3 September 1301 Kaidu was wounded in a serious defeat at Mount Tiejiangu. A follow-up fight with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a>'s reinforcements ended roughly in a draw. Kaidu died late in 1301.</p><p>He appears to have aimed to re-establish the Ulus of Ögedei and assert its control over the Chagatai. His long opposition to the great khans allowed an effectively independent Mongol state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to emerge, and helped the other khanates become independent too. His elder son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapar">Chapar</a> succeeded him, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> forced Chapar to surrender by early 1307, largely ending the Ulus of Ögedei as a power. Kaidu is said to have relied for military advice on his daughter Qutulun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaidu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khanate used this tradition most effectively during the dominance of <b>Kaidu</b> (d. 1301).</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The tension fed the Toluid Civil War with his younger brother Ariq Böke (1260–1264) and the long conflict with his kinsman <b>Kaidu</b> in Central Asia, which helped divide the Mongol world into rival khanates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the throne passed to the Toluids in 1251 they were purged, but Ögedei's grandson <b>Kaidu</b> rebuilt their power from the 1260s.</blockquote><a href="/state/ogedeid-khanate">Ogedeid Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Died in Karakoram, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.88,76.51&amp;year=1277" aria-label="Karakoram on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6480 868 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kaidu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6930 1138h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6930 1138h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.88,76.51&amp;year=1277">What was here in 1277? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
